WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.420
Welcome Andrej, I'm really glad you could join me today

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:07.340
Yeah, thank you for having me

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:13.820
So a lot of people already know your work in deep learning, but not everyone knows your personal story

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.520
So let us instead start by you know telling us. How did you end up doing all this work in deep learning?

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.180
Yeah, absolutely

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.980
So I think my first exposure to deep learning was when I was an undergraduate at the University of Toronto

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.100
And so Geoff Hinton was there and he was teaching a class on deep learning

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.420
And at the time it was restricted boltzmann machines trained on MNIST digits

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.620
And I just really like the way

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.240
Kind of Geoff talked about training the network like the mind of the network and he was using these terms

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.820
and I just thought it was a kind of a flavour of

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.320
something magical happening when when this was training on those digits and

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:50.099
And so that's kind of like my first exposure to it although

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.740
I didn't get into it in a lot of detail at that time

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.800
And then when I was doing my master's degree at the University of British Columbia

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.520
I took a class with Nando de Freitas, and that was again on machine learning

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.580
and that's the first time I kind of dealt kind of deeper into these networks and so on and

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.620
Kind of what was interesting is that I was very interested in artificial intelligence, and so I took classes in artificial intelligence

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:13.559
but a lot of what I was seeing there was just very not satisfying like it was a lot of kind of a

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.420
depth first search breadth first search or alpha-beta pruning all these things I was not understanding how

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.040
like I was not satisfied and so when I was seeing neural networks for the first time

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:27.220
like in machine learning which is kind of this term that I think is more technical and not as well known in kind of

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.540
you know most people talk about artificial intelligence machine learning was more kind of a

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.160
technical term I would almost say.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.000
And so I was dissatisfied with artificial intelligence when I saw machine learning

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.180
I was like this is the AI that I want to kind of spend time on.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.100
This is what's really interesting and that's kind of what took me down those directions.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.980
Is that this is kind of almost a new computing paradigm I would say

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.160
Because normally humans write code, but here in this case

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.160
the optimization writes code and so you're creating input/output specification

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.100
and then you have a lot of examples of it and then the optimization writes code

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.720
and sometimes it can write code better than you

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.660
and so I thought that was just a very new kind of way of thinking about programming and

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.690
That's what kind of intrigued me about it

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:11.340
Then through your work one of the things you've come to be known for

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:17.100
is that you are now the human benchmark for the ImageNet image classification competition

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.120
How did that come about?

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.720
So basically the ImageNet challenge is kind of a

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:24.440
It's sometimes compared to kind of the world cup of computer vision

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.020
So a lot of people kind of care about this benchmark and number— our error rate kind of goes down over time

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.410
And it was not obvious to me kind of where a human would be on the scale

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:36.560
And I've done a similar smaller scale experiment on CIFAR-10 dataset earlier

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:40.420
So what I did in CIFAR-10 is I just was looking at these 32 by 32 images

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.390
And I was trying to classify them myself at the time this was only 10 categories

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:45.669
So it's fairly simple to create an interface for it

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.550
And I think I had an error rate of about 6% on that

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:53.800
And so that was and then based on what I was seeing and how hard task was I think I predicted that

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.380
the lowest error Rate we've achieved would be like

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.060
Okay, I can't remember the exact numbers.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:02.860
I think I guess like 10% and we're now down to like 3 or 2% or something crazy

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.820
So that was my first kind of a fun experiment of like human

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:13.899
baseline and and I thought it was really important for the same purposes that you kind of point out in some of your lectures

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.900
I mean you really want that number to understand

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:22.060
You know it, how well humans are doing so we could compare machine learning algorithms to it and for Imagenet

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.040
It seemed that there was a discrepancy between how important this benchmark was

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.340
and how much focus there was on getting a lower number and us not understanding even how humans

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.160
Are doing on this benchmark, and so I created this Javascript interface

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.080
And I was showing myself the images and then

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.960
the problem with ImageNet is you don't have just ten categories you have a thousand

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.260
And so it almost like a UI challenge of obviously I can't remember a thousand categories

00:03:43.269 --> 00:03:45.849
So how do I make it so that it's something fair?

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:50.019
And so I listed out all the categories, and I gave myself examples of them and so for each image

00:03:50.019 --> 00:03:53.109
I was crawling through a thousand categories and just trying to kind of a

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:54.890
You know see?

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:58.260
Based on the example I was seeing for each category what this image might be and

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:02.440
I thought it was a just an extremely instructed exercise by itself

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.460
I mean I was not I did not understand that like a third of imageNet was dogs and like dog species

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.300
And so that was kind of interesting to see that

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.340
networks spend on unique amount of time caring about dogs. I think a third of its performance comes from dogs

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:21.099
Yeah, so this was kind of something that I did for maybe a week or two

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.529
I put everything else on hold. I thought it was kind of a very fun exercise

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.979
I got a number in the end, and then I thought that one person is not enough

00:04:26.979 --> 00:04:29.469
I wanted to multiple other people and so I was trying to organize

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:35.409
Within a lab to get other people to kind of do the same thing, and I think people are not as willing to contribute

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:39.940
Say like a week or two of like pretty painstaking work

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.240
You know just like yeah sitting down for like five hours and trying to figure out which dog breed

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.620
This is as I was not able to get like enough data in that respect, but we got at least like some

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:55.090
Approximate performance which I thought was was fun, and then this was kind of picked up

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:59.739
And it's uh it wasn't obvious to me at the time. I just watched know the number, but this became like a thing

00:05:00.409 --> 00:05:02.799
And people really like the fact that that this happened

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.680
And I'm referred to jokingly as what the reference human

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.900
and of course that's kind of a hilarious to me. huh, yeah

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:14.050
Well you were you surprised when?

00:05:14.419 --> 00:05:18.669
You know software deep nets finally surpass your performance?

00:05:19.849 --> 00:05:26.919
Absolutely, so yeah, absolutely. I mean especially I mean sometimes. It's really hard to see in the image where it is

00:05:26.919 --> 00:05:32.079
It's just like a tiny blob of like the black black dog is obviously somewhere there, and I'm not seeing like you know

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.550
I'm guessing between like 20 categories and the network just gets it and I don't understand how that comes about

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:39.039
So there's some super humanist to it

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:40.789
but also for the

00:05:40.789 --> 00:05:44.769
I think the network is extremely good at these kind of like statistics of like four types and textures

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:52.750
I just I think in that respect. I was not surprised that the network could better measure those fine statistics across lots of images

00:05:53.389 --> 00:05:54.789
in many cases

00:05:54.789 --> 00:05:59.528
I was surprised because some of the images required you to read like it's just a bottle and you can't see what it is

00:05:59.529 --> 00:06:03.039
But actually tells you what it is in text and so as a human I can read it and fine

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:07.600
But the network would have to learn to read to identify the object because it wasn't obvious just from from it.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:11.320
You know one of the things you become well-known for

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:16.400
in the deep learning community has been grateful to you for has been your teaching the [inaudible] and put that online

