00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.280
the fact that it's

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.380
that it keeps changing on a daily basis

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.100
as people kind of felt like they're on the forefront of something big and I think that's why

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.240
people like really enjoyed that class a lot yeah

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:48.380
And you've really helped a lot of people and handed a lot of hammers

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:54.000
You know as someone there's been in doing deep learning for quite some time now um

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:58.940
The field is evolving rapidly. I'd be curious to hear. How is your own thinking?

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:02.860
How does your understanding of deep learning change over these you know many years?

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:07.420
Yeah, it's basically like when I was seeing restricted boltzmann machines for the first time on digits

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:10.820
It wasn't obvious to me how this technology was going to be used

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:14.179
And how big of a deal it would be and also when I was starting to work on computer vision

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:19.909
Convolutional networks they were around but they were not something that a lot of the computer vision community kind of participated using

00:08:20.849 --> 00:08:26.599
Anytime soon the I think the perception was that this works for small cases, but would never scale for large images

00:08:26.599 --> 00:08:30.589
And that was just extremely incorrect and so

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:37.760
Basically I'm just surprised by how general technology is and how good the results are that was my largest surprise

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:39.760
I would say and it's not only that

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:44.270
So that's one thing that it worked so well and say like ImageNet but the other thing that I think no one saw coming

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:49.279
Or at least for sure I did not see coming is that you can take these pre train networks and that you can transfer

00:08:49.279 --> 00:08:53.599
You can fine-tune them on arbitrary other tasks because now you're not just solving Imagenet and need millions of examples

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.779
This also happens to be very general feature extractor

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:01.660
and I think that's kind of a second insight that I think fewer people saw coming and

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.980
